    'nuances' => array(
        'CTA and action labels are IMPERATIVE PLURAL everywhere, with no exceptions for form or account actions: "Odešlete servisní požadavek", "Zobrazte všechny tiskové zprávy", "Přihlaste se do Ascentialytics", "Připojte soubory (volitelné)". The infinitive is allowed ONLY for pure filter/state labels ("Zobrazit vše") and for aria-labels on close controls ("Zavřít menu"). Never mix the two registers on one page.',
        'Do NOT translate "Tap or click here" as "Klikněte zde" — the touch verb is part of the instruction. Write "Klepněte nebo klikněte zde." (This supersedes the older glossary entry.)',
        'A duration on a "Watch …" CTA describes the VIDEO, and Czech "za několik minut" at the front of a clause means "a few minutes from now", i.e. come back later. Render it as "Během několika minut uvidíte, jak…" or "Podívejte se, jak za pouhých pár minut…" — never "Za několik minut se podívejte…". (This supersedes the older rule that prescribed "Za několik minut se podívejte".)',
        'Keep every technical acronym a buyer would search on, and gloss it rather than replace it: "NDT inspection" = "nedestruktivní zkoušení (NDT)", "NVH" = "NVH (hluk, vibrace a tvrdost jízdy)". Dropping NDT/NVH while keeping OBD, HVAC, ADAS, BaaS and AECD in the same navigation is internally inconsistent.',
        'Never let a prepositional phrase lose its case chain across a coordinated list. "včetně" governs the genitive for EVERY member: "včetně menšího opotřebení či poškození nebo úkolů", never "…nebo úkoly". After any "včetně / bez / kromě / během" list, re-check the ending of the LAST member.',
        'Prefer a subordinate clause to a nominalised prepositional phrase in body copy: "aniž by to ovlivnilo bezpečnost", not "bez ovlivnění bezpečnosti"; "než nastanou", not "před jejich nastáním".',
        'Czech does not use a bare instrumental adverbially the way English uses "In a strategic move, …". Write "V rámci strategického kroku…" or, better, put the subject first: "Společnost X v rámci strategického kroku … oznámila…". Never open a sentence with an unanchored instrumental phrase, and never let a possessive pronoun (její/jeho/jejich) precede the noun it refers to — Czech does not allow cataphora.',
        'Avoid tautological compounds built from the same root or meaning: not "nejnovější novinky" (use "Aktuality" or "Nejnovější zprávy"), not "servisní služby" (use "služby podpory" or "servis a podpora"), not "poradenství a konzultace" in one phrase (pick "poradenství" and keep it site-wide).',
        'Use one Czech word per English concept inside a single line and across the whole site. If the mega-menu says "poradenství a školení OBD" and "služby podpory", the footer must not switch to "konzultace OBD" or "servisní služby".',
        'Do not narrow or broaden a shop name when translating: a "roll shop" is a "brusírna válců" (the roll-grinding shop), NOT a "válcovna" (the entire rolling mill). Before translating a facility or department name, check whether the Czech word names the same part of the plant.',
        'Use the refrigeration-industry term, not a literal one: "vapor compression cooling" = "kompresorové chlazení" (or "chlazení s kompresí par"). Czech "parní" means steam and points at the wrong technology. Likewise "controls" in a machine line-up = "řídicí systémy", not bare "řízení".',
        'Use the established EU/Czech sector term instead of a descriptive paraphrase, because the paraphrase is not searched: "Off-highway & mobile" = "Nesilniční a mobilní technika" (NRMM), not "Technika mimo silniční provoz a mobilní stroje". (This supersedes the older prescription of the long paraphrase.)',
        '"Industries served" = "Odvětví, ve kterých působíme". Do not use "Odvětví, kterým sloužíme" — "sloužit někomu" carries a servant/subordinate colouring a capital-equipment supplier does not want in its main nav. (Supersedes the older rule.)',
        'Never give two different navigation levels the identical label. "About" (section/parent/footer heading) = "O nás"; "Who we are" (the page itself) = "Kdo jsme". Whenever two English labels differ, their Czech renderings must differ too.',
        'Czech does not apposition brand + category the way English does. Join them with the spaced en dash already used in the house style: "Burke Porter – automobilový průmysl", "Burke Porter – systémy ADAS", "Ascential – divize Medical & Life Sciences". Never emit a bare stack like "Burke Porter systémy ADAS".',
        'Turn an English "X as-a-service" service name into a Czech adjective + noun, not a chain of prepositional phrases: "Contract balancing as-a-service (BaaS)" = "Smluvní vyvažování jako služba (BaaS)". The modifier ("contract") must survive as an adjective, not be dropped.',
        '"truck" in an automotive B2B context means commercial vehicles built by the customer, not freight haulage. Write "osobní a nákladní vozidla"; never "nákladní sektor", which names the logistics industry and re-points the enquiry at the wrong audience.',
        '"Whitepaper" = "odborná studie" (or the borrowed "whitepaper"), never "odborná zpráva", which is a technical report. Use the same rendering in the resources hub, in news cards and in any download label. (Supersedes the glossary entry "odborná zpráva".)',
        'Brand and division names in an affiliates/brand list stay in English even when the word looks translatable: "Medical" stays "Medical", not "Zdravotnictví", because the same division is "Medical & Life Sciences" elsewhere on the site.',
        'When declining an English personal name, drop a MUTE final -e before the Czech ending, per ÚJČ: Wilde → Wilda, Shakespeare → Shakespeara, Kime → Kima, Nate → Nata. Only a PRONOUNCED final vowel is kept. Decline by the final sound, and apply the same treatment to every name in one list.',
        'Preserve the specificity of a capability verb. "mine data" = "dolovat data" (data mining), not the generic "analyzovat", which duplicates the analytics claim made elsewhere in the same paragraph. Check every verb in a coordinated series for a distinct Czech equivalent before reusing one.',
        'Do not stack the same preposition twice in one sentence frame ("zavolejte nám na obchodní oddělení na číslo…"). Recast the first branch: "Pro obchodní dotazy nám zavolejte na číslo…".',
        'Keep a modifier attached to the noun it actually modifies: "Equipment model number or type" = "Číslo modelu nebo typ zařízení", not "Číslo modelu zařízení nebo typ"; "Service & Support Products" = "Produkty pro servis a podporu", not the ambiguous genitive stack "Produkty služeb a podpory".',
        'When the English uses two DIFFERENT words in a parallel pair, the Czech must use two different words too: "Elevating support, Enhancing value" = "Vyšší úroveň podpory, větší hodnota", never the same adjective twice.',
        'Number and gender must agree across a coordinated head: "Repair and Field/Remote Service" = "Opravy a terénní/vzdálený servis" — never a plural noun coordinated with a singular one ("Opravy a … služba").',
        'Purpose adjectives derived from verbs take the SHORT -icí ending; the long -ící form is the present participle ("[someone] who is doing it"). Write "školicí kurzy", "chladicí přívěsy", "měřicí systémy", "vyvažovací stroj" — never "Školící kurzy" or "chladící přívěsy". Spell this identically in every string of one site: getting it right in the footer and wrong in the nav is itself the machine-translation fingerprint.',
        'Never carry English spelling into a Czech cognate. Czech is "inovativní" with ONE n, so the superlative is "nejinovativnější", never "nejinnovativnější". Likewise "rebranding" or "přejmenování", never "přebranding"; "modernizace"/"upgrady", never the English plural "upgrades". Any word that looks like its English source with a Czech ending must be checked against a Czech dictionary before it ships.',
        'The reflexive possessive "svůj" may only refer to the subject of its OWN clause. Inside a participial or attributive phrase use "jeho/její/jejich" and recast: "Strategickým krokem, který odráží její závazek…", never "Ve strategickém kroku odrážejícím svůj závazek…". Conversely, when the possessor IS the subject, "svůj" is obligatory: "popište svůj požadavek", never "popište váš požadavek" — non-reflexive "váš" there is the single most reliable non-native marker in Czech.',
        'Under formal vykání every participle, adjective and past-tense form addressed to the reader must be PLURAL and gender-neutral: "které byste mohli sdílet", never "mohl"; "byli byste ochotni", never "byl byste ochoten". Addressing an unknown visitor in the masculine singular is a hard error.',
        'Check subject–verb agreement in every purpose clause. "technici" is masculine animate plural, so "aby podporovali naše zákazníky", never "aby podporovaly". After translating, re-read each "aby …" clause and name its subject out loud.',
        'Translate English idioms functionally, never limb-for-limb. "need a hand" = "potřebujete pomoc" / "potřebujete poradit", never "potřebujete ruku"; "sharp minds" = "bystré hlavy", never "ostré myšlenky" (thoughts cannot be sharp in Czech); "by their side" = "stojíme při nich" / "jsme jim po boku", never "jsme vedle nich"; "get right on it" = "okamžitě se tím začne zabývat", never "vrhne se na to"; "unlock the performance" = "naplno využít výkon", never "odemknout výkon"; "Tap or click here" = "Klikněte zde".',
        'Use the shop-floor Czech term for machinery, never the medical, biological or office homonym: "machine health" = "technický stav stroje" (never "zdravotní stav stroje"); "air handling unit" = "vzduchotechnická jednotka (VZT)" (never "jednotka pro manipulaci se vzduchem"); "turbocharger" = "turbodmychadlo" (never "turbokompresor", which is a different machine class); "actuation" = "akční členy" / "aktuátory" (never "aktuační systémy"); "dyno" = "dynamometr", the machine, not "dynamometrie", the discipline; "asset serial number" = "sériové číslo zařízení" (never "majetku").',
        'Plant-floor location words are not spreadsheet words. In "column, line, cell" a "line" is "výrobní linka" and a "cell" is a "buňka (pracoviště)" — never a spreadsheet "řádek". Before choosing a Czech word for line, cell, column, row or field, decide whether the sentence is about a factory or about a document.',
        'Lock ONE Czech rendering per English term for the whole site and reuse it in nav, tab labels, form options, headings and footer. For this site: "Aerospace & industrials" = "Letecko-kosmický průmysl a průmyslové aplikace"; "Transportation" = "Dopravní technika"; "News" = "Novinky"; "Press" = "Tiskové zprávy"; "end of line" = "konec výrobní linky"; "turbocharger" = "turbodmychadlo"; "aftermarket" = "aftermarket". A tab label that differs from its own nav entry three inches away is the defect a buyer notices first.',
        'Keep parallel UI sets structurally identical. The four contact panels must share one pattern ("Dotaz na prodej – <segment>", "Dotaz na produkt – <segment>"), not four different grammatical shapes. Before delivering, line up sibling items (tabs, bullets, cards, form options) side by side and unify pattern, case and punctuation.',
        '"Resources" as a content section is not "zdroje" (a source, or a raw material). Use "Materiály" for the nav group and "Informační centrum" for a resources hub. Being consistently wrong is still wrong.',
        '"Purpose" in a brand or mission context is "poslání", not "smysl" (which means "a sense" or "a meaning"). "We are driven by purpose" = "Vede nás naše poslání."',
        'Consumer-privacy controls are instructions addressed TO the company, not statements by the visitor. "Do Not Sell or Share My Personal Information" = "Neprodávejte ani nesdílejte mé osobní údaje"; "Limit the Use of My Sensitive Personal Information" = "Omezte používání mých citlivých osobních údajů". A first-person rendering such as "Nechci prodávat své osobní údaje" inverts a legal control and makes the visitor the seller.',
        'CTAs and buttons take the IMPERATIVE PLURAL, never the infinitive: "Zjistěte více", "Přečtěte si více", "Přečtěte si článek", "Založte servisní požadavek", "Promluvte si s odborníkem", "Vyžádejte si demo", "Navštivte centrum", "Zobrazit vše" only where it is a filter label rather than an action. This overrides the older glossary entries "zjistit více" and "Číst dále" — one register for every button on the site, because a page that mixes "Hovořit s odborníkem" and "Hovořte s odborníkem" reads as machine output.',
        'Decline foreign personal names by their final SOUND, not by pasting a Czech ending onto the whole English form: Brunno → "Brunna Morettiho"; Jerome → "Jeroma Stigera"; Jay Drew → "Jaye Drewa"; Erin Dillard → "Erin Dillardovou". Never produce "Brunnoa" or "Jeromea", and never leave one name undeclined while neighbouring names in the same list are declined.',
        'Job titles use the Czech corporate ladder: Senior Vice President = "senior viceprezident" (never "vedoucí viceprezident"), Executive Vice President = "výkonný viceprezident", Chief Technology Officer = "technický ředitel", General Manager = "vedoucí pobočky". Render "Hires X as Y" as "přijímá X do pozice Y" and use that one pattern for every hiring headline in a batch.',
        '"The Americas" is a two-continent region: "Severní a Jižní Amerika". Never "Ameriky" / "v Amerikách" (a calque) and never silently reduce it to the singular "Amerika".',
        'A noun-plus-noun stack copied from English needs a Czech preposition, not a bare genitive: "náklady na selhání", never "náklady selhání"; "požadavky na dodavatelský řetězec", never "požadavky dodavatelského řetězce". After every noun pair, ask which preposition the head noun governs.',
        'Pick the established Czech noun, not an ad-hoc deverbal one. A business "offering" is "nabídka"; "nabídnutí" is the act of offering and cannot name a portfolio. Same test for any -ní/-tí noun you are about to coin.',
        'Match the verb to the object. Events happen: "předvídejte poruchy dříve, než nastanou", never "než se stanou". Machines are made, not built: "tým, který testovací systémy vyrábí / konstruuje", never "staví" (which is for buildings).',
        'If you replace an English idiom, replace it with the SAME claim — never with different content. "Standing on the shoulders of our many brands" is an inherited-capability and portfolio-breadth claim: "Stavíme na odkazu našich mnoha značek". Substituting "Vycházíme z bohaté historie našich značek" deletes "many" and invents a heritage claim the source never made.',
        'Keep the possessive and the object of a product headline. "real-time remote monitoring for your production equipment" = "Vzdálené monitorování vašich výrobních zařízení v reálném čase" — plural, because it is a fleet of machines. Dropping "your" or using the singular "výrobní zařízení" makes it read as the customer\'s facility and removes the product\'s own object from its headline.',
        'Attach a duration or time phrase to the element the source attaches it to. In "Watch how we integrate … in a few minutes" the minutes describe the VIDEO: "Za několik minut se podívejte, jak integrujeme…". Putting it on the integration ("…do systému během několika minut") claims the engineering work takes minutes, which an engineering buyer reads as puffery.',
        'Preserve the strength of a service commitment. "promptly" is "obratem", not "brzy" ("soon"), and B2B support copy stays formal: "neprodleně se tím začne zabývat", never "hned se na to vrhne".',
        'Never narrow a segment or offer name to a subset of what it covers: "Off-highway & mobile" = "Technika mimo silniční provoz a mobilní stroje" (not just construction and agriculture); "Vehicle end-of-line solutions" = "Řešení pro konec výrobní linky vozidel" (not "konečná kontrola", one station); "ADAS aftermarket" = "ADAS pro aftermarket" (a channel, not "náhradní díly"); "Affiliates" = "Přidružené společnosti" (group companies, not "Partneři", which are arm\'s-length); "Industries served" = "Odvětví, kterým sloužíme" (never "obsluhovaná", which is what an operator does to a machine).',
        'In a compound machine name every spec word is load-bearing and must survive: "Core" = "jader", "Rotor" = "rotorů", "High-Speed" = "vysokootáčkový", "Low-Speed" = "nízkootáčkový", and a product designation such as "Polimer" stays as-is. "CMT-TR Polimer Rotor Balancing Machine" = "Vyvažovací stroj rotorů CMT-TR Polimer" — dropping "Rotor" turns a rotor balancer into a machine for polymers, a different product.',
        'Open and close every Czech quotation with the matching pair „ … “, including inside alt text and image descriptions: „A“, not „A„. Check the closing mark of every pair you emit before returning the string.',
        'NUMERAL AGREEMENT: after a number of 5 or more, and after "+" counters, Czech uses the GENITIVE plural: "70+ let inovací", "20+ domovských zemí", "2300+ odborníků", "40+ poboček po celém světě". Nominative plural after a numeral ("70+ roky", "2300+ odborníci") is the classic machine-translation tell.',
        '"mission-critical" is "kriticky důležitý" or "klíčový". Plain "kritický" reads as "in a critical state".',
